  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\mathrm{a} \\in \\mathbf{R}$ and A be a matrix of order $3 \\times 3$ such that $\\operatorname{det}(A)=-4$ and $A+I=\\left[\\begin{array}{lll}1 & a & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]$, where $I$ is the identity matrix of order $3 \\times 3$.<br/>If $\\operatorname{det}((a+1) \\operatorname{adj}((a-1) A))$ is $2^m 3^n, m, n \\in$ $\\{0,1,2, \\ldots .20\\}$, then $\\mathrm{m}+\\mathrm{n}$ is equal to :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "3FgE3",
      "text": "14",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "KUOoM",
      "text": "17",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
